<Docs title="Authorization model & Cedar policies"><div class="doc-title"><span class="section-kicker">CONCEPTS / POLICY</span><h1>Four inputs. One decision.</h1><p>Principal, action, resource, and context make the proposed operation explicit.</p></div><h2>The request model</h2><div class="docs-table"><div><b>Principal</b><span>User or Agent. The HTTP mapper accepts these two subject types; derive the identity in trusted code.</span></div><div><b>Action</b><span>Action::"ToolCall::repo_read". SDK Action.tool("repo_read") produces the matching tool action.</span></div><div><b>Resource</b><span>A domain entity such as Repository::"demo". Define resource types in the schema.</span></div><div><b>Context</b><span>Tool arguments and session facts. AgentGuard flattens args into Cedar’s context alongside session.</span></div></div><h2>Why Cedar</h2><p>A dedicated policy language keeps authorization separate from agent control flow. Cedar’s Rust evaluator and typed schema support explicit grants, prohibitions, and policy validation. AgentGuard adds request wrappers and integration surfaces around that engine.</p><p>No matching permit means deny. A matching forbid overrides a permit. Missing attributes or invalid context can cause evaluation or request errors; do not treat those errors as authorization.</p><h2>Author and validate</h2><Snippet code={policy} title="A narrow permit" lang="text"/><p>The first three clauses constrain who, what, and which object. The when condition requires a trusted MFA fact. Guard optional fields with has. This is the same policy used in the quickstart and browser simulation.</p><Snippet title="Local policy lifecycle" code={'agentguard schema\nagentguard validate\nagentguard sim request.json'}/><p>Schema validation is an explicit authoring step. The authorizer parses stored policies and builds schema-aware requests; do not assume every server startup performs the CLI’s full policy validation.</p><h2>Entities and trusted facts</h2><p>Entity attributes and hierarchy require supplied Cedar entities. The HTTP interface supports an entities array; the CLI supports --entities. Request resource.attrs alone should not be assumed to populate the CLI’s entity store. The quickstart rule uses IDs and context and does not need entity attributes.</p><h2>Policy operations: library scope</h2><p><a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-policy/src/lib.rs">agentguard-policy</a> provides PolicyBundle, BundleRegistry, disk serialization, and version lookup. diff_bundles compares sources, and blast_radius::analyze replays a caller-supplied corpus to classify changed decisions. Coverage depends on that corpus.</p><p>There is no agentguard policy CLI command. Rollback means restoring the selected prior schema/policies and rebuilding or restarting the authorizer. Version selection alone does not activate a bundle in the server.</p><h2>Watcher and hot-reload behavior</h2><p>The filesystem watcher and Unix SIGHUP handler build a complete replacement authorizer and atomically swap it only after policy parsing succeeds. In-flight requests keep their current snapshot; malformed edits leave the last known-good policy set serving and emit a reload error. Embedded Authorizer values remain immutable and must be rebuilt by the embedding application.</p><h2>Decision caching</h2><p>Authorizer::with_cache enables the in-memory LRU/TTL cache. CacheConfig has capacity, allow TTL, deny TTL, and a cache_denies switch. Default values are 10,000 entries, 60 seconds for allows, and 5 seconds for denies. CacheConfig::from_env reads overrides, but standalone run() does not enable this cache.</p><p>Review cache keys and entity handling for your embedding use case: the key serializes AgentRequest and policy version, not the separately supplied Entities set. Ensure identity, context, and entity changes cannot reuse stale authority.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-core/src/authorize/engine.rs">authorizer</a>, <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-server/src/server.rs">server lifecycle</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/schemas/starter.cedarschema">starter schema</a>.</p></Docs>
